{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>He saw all that he does for the people.</b> That he keeps them standing from evening to morning." "Our Rabbi Solomon said: because Moses sits alone while Israel stand, and this is not a proper way of conduct. There is no doubt that Moses' virtue is too great to do thus, for behold Aaron, who was great among all Israel and older than Moses in years — he says to him: בי אדני (Numbers 12:11). And Moses acted the correct way, for the judge sits and the litigants stand, and so it is written: ועמדו שני האנשים אשר להם הריב (Deuteronomy 19:17). And he did not say to him מדוע, only because he sits for judgment alone and has no other judges to assist him. And Rabbi Marinos said that the word מדוע is like מַלֶכֶם (Isaiah 3:15), מַזֶה (above, 4:2), for each is two words, and thus מדוע is מה דעתך — what is your mind — to do thus."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {1 "The comparison is to forms like מַלֶכֶם = מָה לָכֶם and מַזֶּה = מָה זֶה; Ibn Ezra reads מדוע as מָה־דַּעְתְּ, 'what is your mind/understanding.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:38.219092087Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}